  "discussions_data": [
    {
      "id": "d93c040e67963ebb257821da4991064c30951fac",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 460998189,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> The spec is a little vague on this, perhaps they could even be different endianness and there needs be multiple configuration parameters?\n\nWhich spec is that?  The [\"Example for a Serialization Protocol (SOME/IP)\" document for version 4.2.1](http://www.some-ip.com/papers/cache/AUTOSAR_TR_SomeIpExample_4.2.1.pdf) says in section 6.2.2 \"Endianess\" that\n\n> [**TR_SOMEIP_00028**] ⌈ All RPC-Headers shall be encoded in network byte order (big endian) [RFC 791]. The byte order of the parameters inside the payload shall be de- fined by the interface definition (i.e. FIBEX or ARXML) and shall be in network byte order when possible and if no other byte order is specified. ⌋()\n> \n> [**TR_SOMEIP_00029**] ⌈ This means that Length and Type fields shall be always in network byte order. ⌋()\n\nand, at least as I read it, allows both big-endian and little-endian UTF-16, as well as UTF-8, as encodings.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2020-12-05T05:23:59.394Z",
          "updated_at": "2020-12-06T01:50:31.615Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 80704756,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 1140,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 461112304,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "I agree that clearly UTF-16 Big Endian and Little Endian are allowed. I also see that the overall Length parameter in the SOME/IP header (defined in 6.2.3) must be Big Endian. What is ambiguous, at least to me, has to do with the length parameter that can precede variable length strings. The length parameter can be 16 or 32 bits; can that length parameter be Little Endian or Big Endian? \n\nAs I read it, I am not certain if TR_SOMEIP_00029 is referring to only the Length and Type fields in the SOME/IP header (defined in 6.2.3), and allows the length fields that are sometimes optional parts of some parameters (strings, structs, arrays, unions) to be Little Endian. After all, those strings, structs, arrays, and unions are \"parameters inside the payloads\" and those special length fields are parts of those parameters and inside the payload. Also, in that spec \"Length\" is only capitalized when referring specifically to the Length field that measures the length of the payload, defined in 6.2.3.3. The length field inside a dynamic string parameter defined in 6.2.4.4 is not capitalized.\n\nOn the other hand, it might well mean that all length parameters anywhere must be Big Endian.\n\nThe file posted in the issue by the bug reporter had a string in UTF-16 little endian with a 16 bit length parameter proceeding that is also Little Endian. However, it's possible that the poster's sample PCAP is not SOME/IP compliant. (As I noted in the issue, the spec also says that strings should have a BOM, which is not that the case in that sample.)\n\nIf the person who filed the issue has a non-compliant PCAP, then the only thing that needs to change is setting the endianness of the string, which isn't currently used at all.\n\nIf the attached PCAP is (mostly) compliant, then the length parameter of a string can be Little Endian. If so, can the length parameter have endianness different to the string, or does its endianness match that of the string? If it doesn't have to match the endianness of the string, then the configuration entries for string parameters need to have a \"big endian\" field for both the string itself and the length parameter.\n\nThen too, the similar (sometimes optional) length parameter for structs, arrays, and unions also might be able to be Little Endian, and the configuration entry for structs, arrays, and unions would also need to be able to indicate that.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2020-12-06T01:50:31.549Z",
          "updated_at": "2020-12-06T01:50:31.549Z",
